No `.csproj` found, so I can't confirm the target framework. Since this is a class library with **no DI container**, the lightest approach depends on whether you're on .NET 8+.

### Recommended: `TimeProvider` (built-in on .NET 8+)

Inject `TimeProvider` via the constructor — no custom interface needed:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Production** — pass `TimeProvider.System`:
```csharp
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider.System);
```

**Tests** — use `FakeTimeProvider` (from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet):
```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 5, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 4, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 5, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

> **Pre-.NET 8?** Install the `Microsoft.Bcl.TimeProvider` NuGet — same API.

### Alternative: Ambient context (zero constructor changes)

If you don't want to change any constructors at all, an `AsyncLocal`-based static works well for library code:

```csharp
public static class Clock
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<Func<DateTimeOffset>?> s_override = new();
    public static DateTimeOffset UtcNow
        => s_override.Value?.Invoke() ?? TimeProvider.System.GetUtcNow();

    public static IDisposable Override(DateTimeOffset fixedTime)
    {
        s_override.Value = () => fixedTime;
        return new Scope();
    }
    private sealed class Scope : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => s_override.Value = null;
    }
}
```

Then replace `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow` → `Clock.UtcNow`, and in tests:
```csharp
using var _ = Clock.Override(someFixedTime);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(pastDate));
```

`AsyncLocal` ensures parallel tests don't interfere.

---

**I'd recommend the `TimeProvider` constructor-injection route** — it's the standard .NET pattern now, zero custom code to maintain, and `FakeTimeProvider` gives you `Advance()` / `SetUtcNow()` out of the box. Want me to apply the change to `InvoiceCalculator.cs`?
